# scripts/backup.sh must keep LF endings wherever it is checked out.
#
# `core.autocrlf` is true on the machine this is developed on, so the working
# copy of every text file is CRLF locally — and LF on the Linux box that serves
# the app, which checks the same commits out with autocrlf unset. One repository,
# two shapes of every text file, and nothing in a diff shows it.
#
# THIS BLOCK READ "That is harmless for JavaScript and fatal for a shell script"
# UNTIL 2026-09-09. The first half was false and had been for weeks: eight test
# files searched `app/styles.css` and the JSX for text spelled with `\r\n`, so
# 31 assertions passed on the CRLF checkout and failed on the LF one, against a
# tree with nothing wrong in it — a suite that reports the checkout it ran on
# rather than the code. They normalise line endings before matching now, which
# is where the fix belongs; the sentence claiming it could not happen belongs
# here. See the header of `test/adminApproval.test.js`, which had it right first.
#
# AND IT CAME STRAIGHT BACK THE SAME DAY, from the other side — a NINTH file,
# written after the fix and missing it. `test/manualScreen.test.js` matches
# multi-line regexes against the JSX and `app/print.css`, and it went green on
# the tree it was written on, because the first run happened before that tree
# had been checked out again. Five assertions then missed on the next CRLF
# checkout, against a tree with nothing wrong in it: `SECTION_RE` parsed nought
# sections — so the file was checking nothing — and SAID so, which is the only
# reason this was noticed rather than passing vacuously.
#
# So the rule is not closed by having been applied once: a test written after
# the fix is a test written before it, and whichever shape the file is in when
# a suite is FIRST run is the shape it silently depends on. The rule is not
# "normalise when you notice" but "normalise in the `read` helper, before
# anything reads a character" — which is where that file's own `read` got it
# on 2026-09-10.
#
# It is fatal for a shell script: `#!/bin/sh\r` is read by the kernel as an
# interpreter literally named "sh\r", and the whole file fails with
# "bad interpreter: No such file or directory" — a message that names the shebang
# and says nothing about the invisible character in it.
#
# The blob committed here is already LF, so a clone on Linux is fine today. This
# is for the other route: somebody copying the file out of a Windows working
# tree onto a server, which is exactly how a wrapper written for a future Linux
# host would get there.
*.sh text eol=lf

# The same reasoning in reverse. PowerShell tolerates LF, but a .ps1 opened in
# an editor that does not is the sort of thing that gets "fixed" by hand at the
# wrong moment.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
